<!-- The railRF window (railrf.md §11; docs/sonnet-briefs/brief-railrf-7-window.md).

     ── TAKEN FROM THE MATCH DESIGNER, AND "TAKEN" MEANS THE SAME CLASSES ───────────────────────
     §11.1 is explicit: "Not 'inspired by' — the same classes, the same conventions, and where
     possible the same controls." So every style below is MatchDesignerWindow.axaml's style, copied
     with its reasoning intact rather than re-derived: Border.pane over Border.card so a group reads
     as a thing sitting ON the panel; sentence-case headings; label left / value right with the
     numbers in one right-aligned column whether the row is settable or not; ctl:InlineEditText for
     every settable value, with the :disabled dimming that Avalonia does not give a bare TextBlock;
     ListBox.rows for anything that can grow; Slider.compact's negative-margin trick; Button.gridhdr
     left-aligned and unpadded. That window has had roughly ten rounds of refinement and every one of
     them is a decision already made.

     ── THE OPENING SIZE IS THE GOLDEN RATIO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     1199 x 741, the Match Designer's own, for the Match Designer's own reason — and MinWidth is
     1080 rather than 1000 because this window has THREE columns with a canvas between them and a
     specification column that has to be able to show the rail, its reference, its extent, two lists
     and a target. 741 x 1.618 = 1199 to four figures.

     There is deliberately no WindowStartupLocation: the window is shown UNOWNED so it can go behind
     the workspace, and CenterOwner needs an Owner. RailRfWindow.ShowUnowned sets both the location
     mode and the position, off MatchWindowPlacement's arithmetic. -->
<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:rvm="using:CircuitRF.Ui.RailRf"
        xmlns:ctl="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Controls"
        xmlns:conv="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Converters"
        xmlns:ddc="using:CircuitRF.Ui.DataDisplay.Controls"
        xmlns:ddv="using:CircuitRF.Ui.DataDisplay.ViewModels"
        xmlns:ddvw="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Views.DataDisplay"
        xmlns:mi="clr-namespace:Material.Icons.Avalonia;assembly=Material.Icons.Avalonia"
        x:Class="CircuitRF.Ui.Views.RailRf.RailRfWindow"
        x:DataType="rvm:RailRfViewModel"
        Title="{Binding Title}"
        Width="1199" Height="741"
        MinWidth="1080" MinHeight="700"
        CanResize="True">

    <!-- ── DELETE REMOVES THE SELECTED MARKER (owner, 2026-09-19) ──────────────────────────────
         The results plot is a real Data Display PlotControl with real markers on it, and this
         window answered no Delete at all — so a marker added by double-clicking the plot and
         selected by clicking its info box could not be removed by the key every other plot surface
         in the application removes it with. Bound at the WINDOW, like the Match Designer's, so the
         keystroke works wherever focus sits; the plot is not deletable here (the command touches
         markers only — see DeleteSelectedMarkers for why it is not DataDisplay's DeleteSelected).

         Delete ONLY, not Backspace as well — the Match Designer's own note, and this window has far
         more of what it is about: the specification column is InlineEditText rows, focusable at
         rest without being text fields, and a Backspace landing on one would remove a marker the
         user was not looking at. A TextBox that has focus handles Delete itself, so typing is
         unaffected. -->
    <!-- ── Surface 1: the macOS menu bar, hand-mirrored with the in-window Menu below ──────────
         Attached to THIS WINDOW rather than to the application, because NativeMenu.Menu is a
         per-AvaloniaObject attached property and Avalonia does not fall back to an application-scope
         menu for a key window that has none — a railRF window with no menu of its own shows a bare
         app menu on macOS, which is the state this window was in.

         The INSTANCE is fixed for the window's lifetime (src/Ui/CLAUDE.md's own invariant): the
         Window menu's contents are rebuilt by clearing and refilling ITS Items, never by setting a
         second NativeMenu here. -->
    <NativeMenu.Menu>
        <NativeMenu>
            <!-- FILE IS THE TOOLBAR, IN ITS ORDER, and every item binds the command whose hook calls
                 the button's own method (RailRfWindow.InstallMenuHooks). It is a second ROUTE to
                 those operations and never a second implementation.

                 NO Gesture on Save/Save as…/Open/Close, deliberately: a NativeMenuItem's Gesture is
                 functional on macOS and Window.KeyBindings already binds ⌘S / ⇧⌘S / ⌘O / ⌘W there,
                 so spelling them here as well would arm the same keystroke twice. harmonicaRF's and
                 wBond's own File menus take the same shape for the same reason; the in-window items'
                 InputGesture is display-only and cannot. -->
            <NativeMenuItem Header="File">
                <NativeMenuItem.Menu>
                    <NativeMenu>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Open…"         Command="{Binding OpenDocumentCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Save"          Command="{Binding SaveCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Save As…"      Command="{Binding SaveAsCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Import Board…" Command="{Binding ImportBoardCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Report…"       Command="{Binding ReportCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Export…"       Command="{Binding ExportCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Compare…"      Command="{Binding CompareCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Close"         Command="{Binding CloseCommand}"/>
                    </NativeMenu>
                </NativeMenuItem.Menu>
            </NativeMenuItem>

            <NativeMenuItem Header="View">
                <NativeMenuItem.Menu>
                    <NativeMenu>
                        <!-- NO Gesture, deliberately. A NativeMenuItem's Gesture is FUNCTIONAL on
                             macOS, and a bare "F" key equivalent is taken by the menu before any
                             control sees it — including the InlineEditText rows that make up half
                             this window. F belongs to the board canvas, which already answers it;
                             the in-window item's InputGesture is display-only for the same reason. -->
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Zoom to Fit" Command="{Binding ZoomToFitCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <!-- MODE=ONEWAY, and it is not decoration. A checkbox item that wrote BACK
                             would toggle the property itself and the Command would then toggle it a
                             second time — a menu item that does nothing, silently. The tick is a
                             READING of the view model; the Command is the only writer. -->
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Specification" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding ShowSpecification, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        Command="{Binding ToggleSpecificationCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Board" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding ShowBoard, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        Command="{Binding ToggleBoardCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Parts" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding ShowParts, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        Command="{Binding TogglePartsCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Results" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding ShowResults, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        Command="{Binding ToggleResultsCommand}"/>
                        <NativeMenuItemSeparator/>
                        <NativeMenuItem Header="Results Readouts" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding ShowResultText, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        Command="{Binding ToggleResultTextCommand}"/>
                    </NativeMenu>
                </NativeMenuItem.Menu>
            </NativeMenuItem>

            <!-- Populated in the code-behind (RebuildWindowMenu), off the same enumeration the
                 in-window surface uses — NativeMenu has no ItemsSource, which is why WorkspaceWindow
                 builds its own Window menu there too. Its NeedsUpdate is the macOS counterpart of
                 SubmenuOpened, and it is the only hook that fires here: the in-window Menu is hidden
                 on macOS, so its SubmenuOpened never runs at all. -->
            <NativeMenuItem Header="Window">
                <NativeMenuItem.Menu><NativeMenu/></NativeMenuItem.Menu>
            </NativeMenuItem>
        </NativeMenu>
    </NativeMenu.Menu>

    <Window.KeyBindings>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Delete" Command="{Binding DeleteSelectedMarkersCommand}"/>
        <!-- Save, on both spellings, as every other gesture in this application is bound. The
             command is the VIEW MODEL's and it carries no writing of its own: the window installs
             the hook that knows there is a file picker, exactly as it installs PostToUi because it
             is the half that knows there is a dispatcher (wBond and harmonicaRF's own
             SaveDocumentHook). Same command on the buttons, so the gesture and the glyph cannot
             drift apart. -->
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Ctrl+S"       Command="{Binding SaveCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Meta+S"       Command="{Binding SaveCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Ctrl+Shift+S" Command="{Binding SaveAsCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Meta+Shift+S" Command="{Binding SaveAsCommand}"/>
        <!-- The File menu's other two keystrokes, bound HERE and only displayed there — one
             keystroke to one handler, on both platforms. Close goes through OnClosing, so ⌘W asks
             about unsaved work exactly as the window's own close button does. -->
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Ctrl+O"       Command="{Binding OpenDocumentCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Meta+O"       Command="{Binding OpenDocumentCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Ctrl+W"       Command="{Binding CloseCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Meta+W"       Command="{Binding CloseCommand}"/>
    </Window.KeyBindings>

    <Window.Styles>
        <!-- ── The panel language, taken from the Match Designer ──────────────────
             Two tiers, and the whole point is that they are DIFFERENT: a pane is
             SystemChromeMediumLowColor, a card inside it is SystemChromeLowColor, so a group reads
             as a thing sitting ON the panel rather than as a rectangle drawn on it. Both carry the
             one tile border the rest of the application uses, at 6 px corners. -->
        <Style Selector="Border.pane">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="{DynamicResource SystemChromeMediumLowColor}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius"    Value="6"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"          Value="4"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Border.card">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius"    Value="6"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="8,5"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"          Value="0,0,0,5"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- R-rail7-4: the control a refusal names turns RED. One class, worn by whichever control
             the refusal's own RailRefusalControl points at — so the sentence in the status strip and
             the thing to go and fix are never two separate discoveries. -->
        <Style Selector="Border.card.flagged">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1.5"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ComboBox.flagged">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1.5"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.flagged">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1.5"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Anything that can grow is a ListBox, whose default ItemsPanel is a VirtualizingStackPanel
             — the Match Designer's own finding: a bare ItemsControl inside a ScrollViewer is given an
             infinite measure height and realizes EVERY item. A board's parts table is the list here
             that actually reaches that size. These styles flatten the selection chrome so a
             virtualized row reads as a row rather than as something to pick from. -->
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility"   Value="Auto"/>
            <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"                     Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight"                  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"    Value="0"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- THE SELECTED ROW IS VISIBLE. The rule above painted every row transparent in every
             state, selection included, so a list whose "−" button acts on the selection gave no sign
             of what it was about to act on — and with nothing selected the button did nothing at all,
             which reads as a broken button (owner, 2026-09-19). The button is disabled in that state
             now; this is the other half.

             AND THE COLOUR IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME REPORT. This rule first painted the row
             SystemChromeMediumLowColor, which rendered exactly as written and could not be seen: the
             card underneath is SystemChromeLowColor, and under Fluent's LIGHT variant those two keys
             resolve to the SAME colour (#FFF2F2F2) — under dark they are #171717 against #2B2B2B.
             Measured, not inferred. CrfSelectedRowBrush is the accent at CrfDropTargetBrush's own
             opacity, so the row's text stays legible on top of it. -->
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CrfSelectedRowBrush}"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- Hovering the selected row must not wipe the mark off it: Fluent's own ListBoxItem
             control theme paints :selected:pointerover, and that state is more specific than the
             rule above. -->
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem:selected:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CrfSelectedRowBrush}"/>
        </Style>

        <Style Selector="TextBlock.panelhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"     Value="0,0,0,6"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.cardhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"   Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"     Value="0,0,0,2"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.detailLabel">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"          Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"           Value="0.6"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- A label in the Auto column of a label/value row, where the value is a CONTROL rather than
             a right-aligned number. The gap is on the label rather than on the control because the
             control is what stretches: Padding on a ComboBox moves its own text in from its border and
             leaves the border exactly where it was, which is the edge the label was touching (owner,
             2026-09-18). Not on `detailLabel` itself — that class also fills the parts table's seven
             columns, and 8 px of margin in each would come out of the column widths. -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.rowlbl">
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,0"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.detailValue">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"            Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment"   Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.lbl">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"          Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).note">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"     Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"      Value="0.75"/>
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).warn">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"     Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"       Value="0,2,0,0"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"   Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- An observation port's own row. NOT a warning — a load with no current is a correct,
             deliberate state (§2.2), and colouring it red would read as a defect. Dimmed and
             italicised is what says "this row states no number" without saying "this row is wrong". -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.observe">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"            Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="FontStyle"           Value="Italic"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"             Value="0.7"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment"   Value="Center"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- An UNRESOLVED parts row. Dimmed as a whole, because every column of it reads
             "unresolved" — R-rail7-9's own rule is that nothing railRF could not resolve is
             defaulted, so the row is listed and marked rather than filled in or dropped. -->
        <Style Selector="Grid.unresolved">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.55"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A settable value in a label-left/value-right row: same size and edge as detailValue, so
             the settable rows and the read-only ones line up as one column of numbers. -->
        <Style Selector="ctl|InlineEditText.val">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                   Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- A DISABLED settable value has to LOOK disabled, and nothing else in the stack makes it:
             an InlineEditText at rest is a bare TextBlock, and Avalonia's Fluent theme dims neither a
             disabled TextBlock nor a disabled Panel — so a field that was switched off reads exactly
             like one that is on and simply swallows the double-click. -->
        <Style Selector="ctl|InlineEditText:disabled">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.detailLabel:disabled">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ── R-rail7-2, and BOTH halves of it ───────────────────────────────────
             Centred text in comboboxes and on buttons, on the selection AND the items. The ONE
             exception is already an owner decision in the Match Designer and it stays: a
             click-to-sort column header is left-aligned with the column under it, because any
             centring there is a misalignment the user can see. That exception is Button.gridhdr
             below, and a test asserts both halves — the rule and its exception — since a test that
             only checked the rule would delete the exception. -->
        <Style Selector="Button">
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ComboBox">
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ComboBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>

        <Style Selector="Button.gridhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                   Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight"                 Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="0,2"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"            Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"                 Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment"        Value="Stretch"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor"                     Value="Hand"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.gridhdr:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- The add/remove pair on a card header. THE GLYPHS ARE THE ANALYSIS PANEL'S — Plus and
             Delete out of Material.Icons, the same two AnalysesListView puts on its own add and
             remove (owner, 2026-09-19): a bare "−" beside a list does not say "delete this row",
             and the application already had a mark that does. Declared after the bare Button style
             for the ordering reason R-rail7-2's test states. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.rowbtn">
            <Setter Property="Padding"          Value="3,1"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight"        Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth"         Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"       Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.rowbtn mi|MaterialIcon">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource SystemBaseMediumColor}"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.rowbtn:disabled mi|MaterialIcon">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ── A TOOLBAR BUTTON THAT IS LIT WHILE ITS STATE IS IN FORCE ──────────────────────
             LayoutEditorView's own three rules, copied here with their colours intact — the same
             accent fill and white glyph every tool button in the layout editor wears, so a lit
             control means one thing across the application.

             THEY WERE MISSING AND ONE BUTTON WAS ALREADY ASKING FOR THEM: the board panel's
             magnifier does `Classes.Set("ToolActive", …)` in the code-behind, and with no style of
             that name in this window the class was set on every arm and painted nothing. So the
             zoom box has always armed invisibly here. A class nobody styles is not a no-op, it is
             a feature that silently does not exist. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background">
                <Setter.Value><SolidColorBrush Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColor}"/></Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background">
                <Setter.Value><SolidColorBrush Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorLight1}"/></Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- A tab in either strip: the board panel's four maps, and the results panel's two halves.
             Only the metrics — the lamp is Button.ToolActive's, above, which is the point of them
             being toolbar buttons at all.

             A Button and not a ToggleButton or a TabControl. Both strips switch a property the view
             model owns (which OVERLAY, which results half) and neither carries content of its own,
             so a TabControl would be a content host with nothing in it; and a ToggleButton toggles
             ITSELF on click, so clicking the selected tab turned the strip off and left the panel
             showing something no tab claimed — a state SyncTabs had to re-assert against on every
             click. A lamp set from the view model's property cannot reach it at all. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.tabbtn">
            <Setter Property="Padding"   Value="6,3"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth"  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0"/>
        </Style>

    </Window.Styles>

    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto">

        <!-- ── Menu bar and title bar ────────────────────────────────────────
             THE TWO SHARE ROW 0 rather than the menu taking a row of its own, so nothing else in
             this window has to be renumbered and the marker-info overlay's RowSpan still covers
             everything. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="0">

        <!-- ── railRF's OWN MENU BAR (owner, 2026-09-19) ────────────────────────────────────────
             This window had NO menu at all. Two things followed from that and both were reported:
             there was no Zoom to Fit anywhere but the board panel's own toolbar — which is hidden
             with that panel — and there was no way back to the circuitRF windows this one was
             opened from, because railRF is shown UNOWNED so it can go behind them.

             TWO SURFACES, HAND-MIRRORED, exactly as WorkspaceWindow, HarmonicaMenuView and
             WBondMenuView already maintain theirs: the macOS NativeMenu attached to this Window
             (below), and this in-window Menu, which macOS hides because the native bar carries it.
             Anything added here must go on BOTH, or it exists on one platform only.

             THREE MENUS. File is a second ROUTE to the toolbar below it — every item binds the
             command whose hook calls that button's own method, so there is one implementation of
             Open, of Export and of Compare, not two. View and Window are what had no home at all:
             Zoom to Fit lived only on the board panel's toolbar, which goes with that panel, and
             nothing anywhere led back to the circuitRF windows this one was opened from. -->
        <Menu Name="RailMenuBar" IsVisible="{OnPlatform True, macOS=False}">
            <MenuItem Header="_File" Padding="8,4">
                <!-- InputGesture is DISPLAY ONLY (Window.KeyBindings is what actually binds these),
                     which is what keeps one keystroke to one handler — see the native surface's note. -->
                <MenuItem Header="_Open…" Command="{Binding OpenDocumentCommand}"
                          InputGesture="Ctrl+O"
                          ToolTip.Tip="A .crail, or a .clay layout from any workspace. The layout's own technology reference is what supplies the stackup."/>
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Save" Command="{Binding SaveCommand}" InputGesture="Ctrl+S"/>
                <MenuItem Header="Save _As…" Command="{Binding SaveAsCommand}" InputGesture="Ctrl+Shift+S"/>
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Import Board…" Command="{Binding ImportBoardCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="The artwork, and the placement, BOM and netlist that go with it."/>
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Report…" Command="{Binding ReportCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="The drop, the breakdown, the via flags and what they were computed at, as a page drawn by the same renderers the window draws with."/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Export…" Command="{Binding ExportCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="CSV, .npy, .mat, .txt, or the report page as SVG or PDF — the extension picks the format, exactly as circuitrf rail -o does."/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Compare…" Command="{Binding CompareCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="This design against another .crail — the one that is known to work."/>
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Close" Command="{Binding CloseCommand}" InputGesture="Ctrl+W"/>
            </MenuItem>

            <MenuItem Header="_View" Padding="8,4">
                <!-- InputGesture is DISPLAY ONLY in Avalonia (HotKey is the functional one), which
                     is exactly what is wanted: F is the board canvas's own key and this label must
                     not become a second handler for it. -->
                <MenuItem Header="Zoom to _Fit" Command="{Binding ZoomToFitCommand}"
                          InputGesture="F"
                          ToolTip.Tip="Frame the whole board. The same gesture the board panel's own button and its F key run — here as well because that toolbar goes with the panel."/>
                <Separator/>
                <!-- THE SAME FIVE COMMANDS THE TOOLBAR LAMPS RUN, never a second copy of the
                     state. Each is a CheckBox against the property its button lights from, so the
                     menu and the lamp cannot disagree; the last showing panel's item dims itself,
                     because the Command's own CanExecute is what dims the button. -->
                <!-- MODE=ONEWAY — see the native surface's note: the tick READS the view model and
                     the Command is the only writer, so a click cannot toggle it twice. -->
                <MenuItem Header="_Specification" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                          IsChecked="{Binding ShowSpecification, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Command="{Binding ToggleSpecificationCommand}"/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Board" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                          IsChecked="{Binding ShowBoard, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Command="{Binding ToggleBoardCommand}"/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Parts" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                          IsChecked="{Binding ShowParts, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Command="{Binding TogglePartsCommand}"/>
                <MenuItem Header="_Results" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                          IsChecked="{Binding ShowResults, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Command="{Binding ToggleResultsCommand}"/>
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="Results _Readouts" ToggleType="CheckBox"
                          IsChecked="{Binding ShowResultText, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Command="{Binding ToggleResultTextCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="The text cards under the results plot. Off, the plot takes the height they were using."/>
            </MenuItem>

            <!-- Every open circuitRF window, selectable to bring it to the front. Contents are
                 rebuilt just before either surface opens — window lifetime and dirty state both
                 change constantly, and the menu is only ever read at the moment it is shown. It is
                 SEEDED at construction as well, because an Avalonia MenuItem reports HasSubMenu
                 from its item count: an empty ItemsSource makes this a leaf, and SubmenuOpened then
                 never fires at all. That is a self-latching dead menu and WorkspaceWindow already
                 shipped it once. -->
            <MenuItem Name="WindowMenuItem" Header="_Window" Padding="8,4"
                      SubmenuOpened="OnWindowMenuOpened"/>
        </Menu>

        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="8,6">
            <!-- ── THE FILE NAME, ITS PATH AND A WAY TO GO THERE (owner, 2026-09-19) ────────────
                 The title carries the extension, so what is on screen is the name of a FILE. Its
                 tooltip is the full path, which is the half a title cannot say — on a machine with
                 this board in three places, which copy is the whole question. And right-clicking it
                 reveals that file in the platform's own file manager, which is the same item,
                 spelled by the same platform test, that the project tree and the document tabs
                 already carry (FileReveal).

                 THE FILE NAME AND NOTHING ELSE (owner, 2026-09-19). "railRF —" stays on the OS
                 title bar, where the window is one row among every window on the machine; in the
                 window it is a word already read, over content that could not be anything else.
                 DocumentLabel is that half; Title, on the Window above, is still the full one. -->
            <TextBlock Name="TitleText" Text="{Binding DocumentLabel}" FontSize="14" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                       ToolTip.Tip="{Binding DocumentPathTip}">
                <TextBlock.ContextMenu>
                    <!-- ONE ContextMenu instance, filled on Opening — the shape the board canvas
                         below already uses, and for the same reason: a ContextMenu is a popup with
                         its own visual root, so items built fresh per right-click stack up. -->
                    <ContextMenu Name="TitleContextMenu" Opening="OnTitleContextMenuOpening"/>
                </TextBlock.ContextMenu>
            </TextBlock>
            <!-- Icons, not words — the schematic editor's toolbar shape, which the Match Designer
                 already took: a bare MaterialIcon at 16 px in a Padding="6,3" button, with the word
                 that would have been on the face moved into ToolTip.Tip.

                 NO CLOSE BUTTON. The window's own title bar already carries one, and a second in the
                 client area is a second thing to aim at that does the same job (owner, 2026-08-20). -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                <!-- ── THE FOUR PANEL LAMPS (owner, 2026-09-19) ────────────────────────────────
                     Four toggles, one per panel, so a reader can give one of them the whole window
                     for a moment. They are ORDERED AS THE WINDOW IS — specification on the left,
                     the board and the parts table under it in the middle, results on the right —
                     and the glyphs say the same thing a second time: Material's own page-layout
                     family draws the sidebar, the body and the footer of a page, which is exactly
                     the shape each button turns off.

                     LIT MEANS ON SCREEN, on Button.ToolActive's accent — the same lamp the map
                     strip, the results strip and the Accuracy button wear, so a lit control means
                     one thing in this window. All four start lit.

                     Plain Buttons bound to Classes.ToolActive rather than ToggleButtons, for the
                     reason the tab-strip comment below gives at length: a ToggleButton toggles
                     ITSELF, so its visual state and the view model's would be two copies of one
                     fact. Here the view model owns it and the class follows it.

                     AT LEAST ONE IS ALWAYS SHOWING, and the last lit button is DISABLED rather
                     than inert — the Command's own CanExecute dims it, so there is no IsEnabled
                     here to keep in step. Four hidden panels is a window with nothing between its
                     toolbar and its status strip. -->
                <Button Name="SpecificationPaneButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ShowSpecification}"
                        Command="{Binding ToggleSpecificationCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Specification — show or hide the left column. Hidden, the board, the parts table and the results take the width it releases; it never takes more than its own. At least one panel is always showing, so the last one cannot be hidden.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="PageLayoutSidebarLeft" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="BoardPaneButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ShowBoard}"
                        Command="{Binding ToggleBoardCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Board — show or hide the artwork and its map strip. Hidden, the parts table takes the whole centre column. At least one panel is always showing, so the last one cannot be hidden.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="PageLayoutBody" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="PartsPaneButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ShowParts}"
                        Command="{Binding TogglePartsCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Parts — show or hide the parts table. Hidden, the board expands down into its space. At least one panel is always showing, so the last one cannot be hidden.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="PageLayoutFooter" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="ResultsPaneButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ShowResults}"
                        Command="{Binding ToggleResultsCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Results — show or hide the right column. Hidden, the board and the parts table take the width it releases. At least one panel is always showing, so the last one cannot be hidden.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="PageLayoutSidebarRight" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>

                <!-- The layout editor's own toolbar rule, and it is doing the layout editor's job:
                     these four change what is SHOWN, the three to its right act on the document. -->
                <Border Width="1" VerticalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="2,3"
                        Background="{DynamicResource SystemControlForegroundBaseLowBrush}"/>

                <!-- OPEN, and it is not a second import. An import CREATES a cell from foreign
                     artwork; this points the window at a document or a layout that already exists —
                     a `.crail` anywhere on disk, or a `.clay` in any workspace, whose own `.ctech`
                     reference is what supplies the stackup. It is left of Import because it is the
                     cheaper of the two and the one a returning user wants. -->
                <Button Name="OpenButton" Padding="6,3"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Open — a .crail, or a .clay layout from any workspace. The layout's own technology reference is what supplies the stackup.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FolderOpenOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <!-- ── SAVE, WHICH THIS WINDOW HAD NEVER HAD (owner, 2026-09-19) ────────────────
                     `RailDocumentIo` has been able to write a `.crail` since brief 1 and nothing in
                     the application called it, so every edit this window makes lived for as long as
                     the window did. Beside Open because they are the pair; left of Import because
                     Import CREATES and these two are about the document that exists.

                     ⌘S / Ctrl+S and ⇧⌘S / Ctrl+Shift+S are bound at the window, and the title
                     carries a bullet while the document differs from disk. Save is ALWAYS enabled:
                     the bullet is information, a disabled Save would be a refusal. -->
                <Button Name="SaveButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Command="{Binding SaveCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Save (⌘S) — the rails, the sources and loads, the targets, the settings and which panels are open. A window that has never been saved asks where.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="ContentSaveOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="SaveAsButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Command="{Binding SaveAsCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Save as… (⇧⌘S) — write this document under a new name. A file another railRF window is already holding is a refusal, not two working copies of it.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="ContentSaveEditOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="ImportButton" Padding="6,3"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Import a board — the artwork, and the placement, BOM and netlist that go with it">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FileImportOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <!-- DISABLED WITH A REASON rather than silently inert. A control that looks live and
                     does nothing is indistinguishable from one that is broken; one that is dimmed and
                     says what it is waiting for is not. -->
                <Button Name="ReportButton" Padding="6,3"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanExport}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Report — the drop, the breakdown, the via flags and what they were computed at, as a page drawn by the same renderers the window draws with.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FileDocumentOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <!-- §11.2: "Clean and simple by default." The reference-plane options, the via
                     limit basis, the meshing density and the temperature note live HERE and nowhere
                     else — that is the whole list, and nothing has been added to it. The status strip
                     states the ones in force on every frame, which is the half that has to be
                     unmissable; the CONTROLS do not. -->
                <Button Name="SettingsButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.flagged="{Binding IsStackupFlagged}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Settings — reference-plane extent, via limit basis, meshing density and the temperature everything is computed at">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="CogOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                    <Button.Flyout>
                        <Flyout Placement="BottomEdgeAlignedRight">
                            <StackPanel Width="320" Spacing="6">
                                <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Settings"/>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Reference extent"/>
                                    <!-- The item template is what keeps `AsImported` off the face of
                                         the window. The enum is still what is bound and still what is
                                         persisted — a display name that reached the `.crail` would be
                                         a second spelling of a value the file format already has. -->
                                    <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" FontSize="10" MinHeight="0" Padding="6,1"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                              ItemsSource="{Binding ReferenceExtents}"
                                              SelectedItem="{Binding ReferenceExtent, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                              ToolTip.Tip="What the reference conductor is taken to be. Two of the three are OPTIMISTIC, and the status strip says which is in force on every frame.">
                                        <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate>
                                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Converter={x:Static conv:RailReferenceExtentNameConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ComboBox>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Via plating"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViaPlatingEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        Watermark="from the stackup"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The plated barrel thickness a via's current limit is computed from. EMPTY IS NOT A DEFAULT — the stackup's own via entry is read first where it states one, and every flag says which basis produced it."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Via rise"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViaTemperatureRiseEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The temperature RISE a via's current limit is stated at. A budget, not a temperature: there is no thermal model in railRF and nothing else reads this."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Temperature"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                        Text="{Binding TemperatureEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="What every resistance in this document is computed at. railRF is not a thermal tool and does not derate — the report states this number so a reader knows which basis they have."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Mesh cell"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                        Text="{Binding MeshCellEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        Watermark="automatic"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="How finely Accuracy meshes, as a cell size. EMPTY IS THE ORDINARY CASE and not a missing value — the extractor computes a cell size from the artwork, and a number here overrides it. Read by the accurate reading only; the fast one traces instead."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <TextBlock Classes="note"
                                           Text="Every one of these is stated on the report, which is why they are document state rather than session state."/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Flyout>
                    </Button.Flyout>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="HelpButton" Padding="6,3"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Help — the railRF chapter of the circuitRF reference">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="HelpCircleOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ── The three columns (§11.3) ──────────────────────────────────── -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1" Name="PaneGrid" ColumnDefinitions="300,*,340">

            <!-- ══ SPECIFICATION ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Name="SpecificationPane" Classes="pane"
                    IsVisible="{Binding ShowSpecification}">
                <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto">
                    <TextBlock Classes="panelhdr" Text="Specification"/>

                    <!-- The rail card. The SELECTOR is above the lists and it moves the WHOLE window
                         — that is §11.3's fourth point, and it is why it sits at the top of this pane
                         rather than beside the results. -->
                    <Border Grid.Row="1" Classes="card"
                            Classes.flagged="{Binding IsRailSelectorFlagged}">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                            <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Rail"/>

                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Rail"/>
                                <!-- ItemsSource BEFORE SelectedItem, always. Declared in that order
                                     here and built in that order in the view model: a combo whose
                                     ItemsSource binding attaches after the selection was set drops it
                                     silently, which is the wBond round-6 blank-Group-combo bug. -->
                                <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" Name="RailSelector" FontSize="10"
                                          MinHeight="0" Padding="6,1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                          ItemsSource="{Binding Rails}"
                                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedRailName, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                          ToolTip.Tip="Which rail this window is showing. One rail is analysed at a time; the set is solved in dependency order."/>
                            </Grid>

                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Ref."/>
                                <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" Name="ReferenceSelector" FontSize="10"
                                          MinHeight="0" Padding="6,1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                          Classes.flagged="{Binding IsReferenceLayerFlagged}"
                                          ItemsSource="{Binding ReferenceLayerOptions}"
                                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedReferenceLayer, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                          ToolTip.Tip="The reference return's drawing layer. railRF proposes one and never assumes it."/>
                            </Grid>

                            <!-- The PROPOSAL and its REASON, together. A proposal with no reason is
                                 indistinguishable from a default, and a default here is the guess
                                 Q-8 closed. -->
                            <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding ReferenceProposalReason}"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding !IsReferenceConfirmed}"/>
                            <Button Content="Confirm reference" FontSize="10" Padding="8,2"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                    Command="{Binding ConfirmReferenceCommand}"
                                    IsVisible="{Binding !IsReferenceConfirmed}"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Run stays disabled until the reference has been affirmatively set — the one place this window deliberately costs a click."/>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Border>

                    <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="0">

                            <!-- ── Step 2: PICKING the rail (§2.3, R-rail7-8) ──────────
                                 With a board file or a board netlist, pick the power net from a list.
                                 OTHERWISE click the pour — geometry alone has no net in it and railRF
                                 will not guess a net name, so that route makes a rail anchored by a
                                 coordinate instead. Either way, what happens next is the same:
                                 PdnRailRegions walks everything galvanically connected to the pick,
                                 through vias and across layers, and the board shows whether the rail
                                 is one region or three islands joined by a 20 mil neck.

                                 The card is here only while there is something to pick FROM. On a
                                 document whose rails are already set it is a control with nothing to
                                 do, and a list of every net on the board standing open above the
                                 sources would read as a second rail selector. -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding HasPickableNets}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Pick the rail"/>
                                    <ListBox Name="NetList" Classes="rows" MaxHeight="110"
                                             ItemsSource="{Binding AvailableNets}"
                                             SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedNet, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                        <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="x:String">
                                                <TextBlock Classes="lbl" Text="{Binding}"/>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ListBox>
                                    <!-- NOT FULL WIDTH, AND NOT LIVE WITH NOTHING PICKED (owner,
                                         2026-09-19). A stretched button reads as the card's own
                                         header rather than as the action on the list above it, and
                                         one that is enabled while nothing is highlighted answers a
                                         press with silence. Its face also says which of the two
                                         things it will do, because a net that is already a rail is
                                         SELECTED rather than added — see PickRailButtonText. -->
                                    <Button Content="{Binding PickRailButtonText}"
                                            FontSize="10" Padding="10,2"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                                            Command="{Binding PickSelectedNetCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="The rail takes the net's own name. It states no reference layer — that is the next step, and it is confirmed rather than assumed."/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- Where there is neither a board file nor a netlist, the pick is made on
                                 the board itself. The GESTURE is the canvas's; this says so, because a
                                 pane with no pick list and no sentence reads as a missing feature. -->
                            <TextBlock Classes="note" Margin="0,0,0,6"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding HasNoPickableNets}"
                                       Text="No board netlist named any nets, so pick the rail by clicking its pour on the board. railRF will not guess a net name."/>

                            <!-- ── Sources: a LIST, with add and remove ───────────────── -->
                            <Border Classes="card">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Sources" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,2,2"
                                                Command="{Binding AddSourceCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Add a source. A rail can be fed by a cell through a connector, by a regulator's output, or by both. The new row states no anchor — double-click its first column and type the refdes and pin.">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Plus" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="2" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,0,2"
                                                Command="{Binding RemoveSourceCommand}"
                                                CommandParameter="{Binding SelectedSource}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Remove the selected source">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Delete" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <ListBox Name="SourceList" Classes="rows" MaxHeight="120"
                                             ItemsSource="{Binding Sources}"
                                             SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedSource, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                        <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="rvm:RailSourceRowViewModel">
                                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,1">
                                                    <!-- THE ANCHOR IS SETTABLE (owner, 2026-09-19).
                                                         It was a TextBlock, and nothing else in the
                                                         window named a refdes or a pin — so "+" made
                                                         a row reading "(no anchor)" that could never
                                                         be given one. Same control and same contract
                                                         as every other value on this window; the
                                                         candidates this board offers are on its
                                                         tooltip, from the netlist the solve will
                                                         resolve against. -->
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Classes="val" MinWidth="56"
                                                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                                        Text="{Binding AnchorEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        Watermark="refdes.pin"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="{Binding AnchorTip}"/>
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" MinWidth="52"
                                                                        Text="{Binding VoltageEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="Open-circuit voltage. Empty is NOT zero — a source with no stated level is one the frequency answer can use and the DC answer reports."/>
                                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="⚑" FontSize="11" Margin="3,0,0,0"
                                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"
                                                               IsVisible="{Binding IsFlagged}"
                                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Refusal}"/>
                                                </Grid>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ListBox>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── Loads: a LIST, and a row with no current reads observe ── -->
                            <Border Classes="card">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Loads" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,2,2"
                                                Command="{Binding AddLoadCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Add a load. A new row states no current, which makes it an observation port until one is typed — and no anchor, which you give it by double-clicking its first column and typing the refdes and pin.">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Plus" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="2" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,0,2"
                                                Command="{Binding RemoveLoadCommand}"
                                                CommandParameter="{Binding SelectedLoad}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Remove the selected load">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Delete" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <ListBox Name="LoadList" Classes="rows" MaxHeight="150"
                                             ItemsSource="{Binding Loads}"
                                             SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedLoad, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                        <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="rvm:RailLoadRowViewModel">
                                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,1">
                                                    <!-- Settable, for the reason the source row's
                                                         own comment gives. -->
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Classes="val" MinWidth="56"
                                                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                                        Text="{Binding AnchorEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        Watermark="refdes.pin"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="{Binding AnchorTip}"/>
                                                    <!-- The SAME right edge whether the row states a
                                                         current or reads "observe", so the column of
                                                         numbers stays one column. -->
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" MinWidth="52"
                                                                        Text="{Binding CurrentEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The DC current this port draws. Clear it to make the row an observation port — which is what a row with no current IS, not a zero."/>
                                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="⚑" FontSize="11" Margin="3,0,0,0"
                                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"
                                                               IsVisible="{Binding IsFlagged}"
                                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Refusal}"/>
                                                </Grid>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ListBox>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── Target ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
                            <Border Classes="card">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Target"/>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Drop"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding DropBudgetEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The DC drop budget. In millivolts, because a budget of 0.08 V reads as a typo where 80 mV does not."/>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Z"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding ImpedanceTargetEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The flat impedance target, in milliohms. A per-port mask lives on the load row instead."/>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Band from"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding BandStartEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="Band to"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding BandStopEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── Aggressors: an INPUT, not a decoration (§2.2) ────────
                                 A PDN peak matters if something on the board excites it, and this list is
                                 the only input that knows whether it does — so it belongs with the other
                                 inputs, under Target, and not beside the parts table (owner, 2026-09-19).
                                 It sat in the board column because both it and the parts table are about
                                 what is ON the board; what that cost was a third of the height of the one
                                 panel in this window that is a PICTURE, to a list of at most a few rows. -->
                            <Border Classes="card">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto,Auto">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Aggressors" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="from the BOM" FontSize="10"
                                                Padding="6,0" MinHeight="0" Margin="0,0,4,2"
                                                IsVisible="{Binding HasRecognisedAggressors}"
                                                Command="{Binding AcceptRecognisedAggressorsCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Add every crystal and converter the BOM reader recognised. Each row says it came from the BOM — a pre-filled frequency nobody checked is exactly the one that will be wrong."/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="2" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,2,2"
                                                Command="{Binding AddAggressorCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Add an aggressor line by hand. The new row states nothing — double-click its name, its frequency or its harmonic count and type.">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Plus" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="3" Classes="rowbtn" Margin="0,0,0,2"
                                                Command="{Binding RemoveAggressorCommand}"
                                                CommandParameter="{Binding SelectedAggressor}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Remove the selected aggressor">
                                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Delete" Width="13" Height="13"/>
                                        </Button>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <ListBox Name="AggressorList" Classes="rows" MaxHeight="70"
                                             ItemsSource="{Binding Aggressors}"
                                             SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedAggressor, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                        <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="rvm:RailAggressorRowViewModel">
                                                <!-- ── EVERY VALUE ON THIS ROW IS SETTABLE (owner, 2026-09-19)
                                                     It was one TextBlock bound to Summary, so "+" made a row
                                                     nobody could name or tune: the view model had Name,
                                                     FrequencyEntry and Harmonics and nothing on screen reached
                                                     any of them, and a double-click on a TextBlock opens no
                                                     editor. Same control and same contract as the source and
                                                     load rows above — InlineEditText, which is a TextBlock at
                                                     rest and a TextBox once double-clicked, and whose refused
                                                     value snaps back because the row notifies either way. -->
                                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,1">
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Classes="val" MinWidth="40"
                                                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                                        Text="{Binding Name, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        Watermark="what it is"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="What is doing the exciting — &quot;32 kHz xtal&quot;, &quot;converter&quot;. It names the row on the plot and in the coincidence list."/>
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" MinWidth="52"
                                                                        Text="{Binding FrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The fundamental. A frequency pre-filled from the BOM that nobody checked is exactly the one that will be wrong — so check it here."/>
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="2" Classes="val" MinWidth="26"
                                                                        Margin="3,0,0,0"
                                                                        Text="{Binding HarmonicsEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="How many harmonics to draw and check. 1 is the fundamental alone; a converter&apos;s fifth landing on a 3 dB peak is the sentence this list exists to produce."/>
                                                    <!-- WHERE IT CAME FROM, on the row's own face. -->
                                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Classes="note" Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                               Text="{Binding OriginText}"/>
                                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Text="⚑" FontSize="11" Margin="3,0,0,0"
                                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"
                                                               IsVisible="{Binding IsFlagged}"
                                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Refusal}"/>
                                                </Grid>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ListBox>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── The regulator offer (§2.3) ──────────────────────────── -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding HasRegulatorOffers}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Next rail"/>
                                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding RegulatorOffers}">
                                        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="rvm:RailRegulatorOffer">
                                                <StackPanel Spacing="2" Margin="0,2">
                                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding Summary}"/>
                                                    <Button Content="Add it" FontSize="10" Padding="8,1"
                                                            HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                                            Command="{Binding $parent[Window].((rvm:RailRfViewModel)DataContext).AcceptRegulatorOfferCommand}"
                                                            CommandParameter="{Binding}"/>
                                                </StackPanel>
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl>
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note"
                                               Text="Offered, not created — the rail chain is a modelling statement and it is yours."/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                        </StackPanel>
                    </ScrollViewer>
                </Grid>
            </Border>

            <!-- ══ BOARD ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- THE BOARD AND THE PARTS TABLE SHARE THIS COLUMN, which is why hiding one of
                 them widens nothing and hiding BOTH is what collapses the column. RailRfWindow's
                 SyncPanes owns the arithmetic; what is here is which parts of it are drawn. -->
            <Border Grid.Column="1" Name="BoardPane" Classes="pane"
                    IsVisible="{Binding ShowBoardColumn}">
                <Grid Name="BoardPaneGrid" RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
                    <Grid Name="BoardPaneHeader" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*"
                          IsVisible="{Binding ShowBoard}">
                        <TextBlock Classes="panelhdr" Text="Board" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <!-- THE SAME FOUR BUTTONS THE LAYOUT EDITOR CARRIES, in the same order and
                             with the same tooltips (§11.6). The keyboard is not the only route to any
                             of these, and the buttons are where a user who has never read a tooltip
                             finds them. Each one calls the CANVAS’s own method and hands the keyboard
                             back to it — see LayoutEditorView.OnZoomToFit for why the focus return is
                             part of the gesture rather than a nicety. -->
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="8,0,0,6"
                                    IsVisible="{Binding HasBoard}">
                            <Button Name="BoardZoomToFitBtn" Click="OnBoardZoomToFit"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Zoom to Fit  (F)" Padding="6,3">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FitToPage" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Name="BoardZoomBoxBtn" Click="OnBoardZoomBoxTool"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Zoom Box  (Z) — drag a box to zoom to it  (Esc cancels; Ctrl+ +/- steps)"
                                    Padding="6,3">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="MagnifyPlusOutline" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Name="BoardZoomOutBtn" Click="OnBoardZoomOut"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Zoom Out" Padding="6,3">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="MagnifyMinusOutline" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Name="BoardZoom1To1Btn" Click="OnBoardZoom1To1"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Zoom 1:1 (1 px per display-unit tick)" Padding="6,3">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Magnify" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <!-- The stackup this board is priced against, in its own editor (owner,
                                 2026-09-19). It is beside the zoom buttons because the question that
                                 sends a user there is usually about the PICTURE — a layer that should
                                 not be drawn — and the answer is a Vis box in that file. Hidden rather
                                 than disabled where the board resolved no `.ctech` path: there is no
                                 file to open, and a dead button invites a click that says nothing. -->
                            <Button Name="BoardEditTechBtn" Click="OnBoardEditTechnology"
                                    IsVisible="{Binding HasTechnologyFile}"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="{Binding EditTechnologyTip}" Padding="6,3"
                                    Margin="6,0,0,0">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="LayersTriple" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <!-- The tab strip switches the OVERLAY and NEVER the geometry (§11.3). The
                             artwork underneath is the same on all four; brief 8 draws each map. -->
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Name="OverlayTabs" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                    Spacing="2" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,0,0,6">
                            <!-- ── FOUR TOOLBAR BUTTONS, not four word tabs (owner, 2026-09-19) ──
                                 The same shape as the zoom row to their left and as every tool
                                 button in the layout editor: a 14 px Material glyph in a
                                 Padding="6,3" button, the word moved onto the tooltip, and
                                 Button.ToolActive's accent lamp for the one in force. A Button and
                                 not a ToggleButton for the reason SyncTabs already had to work
                                 around — a ToggleButton toggles ITSELF on click, so clicking the
                                 selected tab turned the strip off and left the board showing an
                                 overlay no tab claimed. The lamp is set from the view model's own
                                 property, so the strip cannot reach a state the view model does not
                                 name. -->
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="CopperTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Copper — the artwork as drawn, with this rail's own copper highlighted">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="LayersOutline" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="DropTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Drop — the artwork coloured by node voltage">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="GradientVertical" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="ImpedanceTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="|Z| — how many ohms the plane presents between the chosen load pin and every point on the board, at ONE frequency. Not a sweep: the curve over frequency is on the Frequency results tab.">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Omega" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="ClassTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Class — which copper the fast model treated as a trace and which it meshed. A requirement, not a diagnostic: a wide supply polygon mistaken for a trace is optimistic and invisible.">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="ShapeOutline" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>

                    <!-- THE LAYOUT EDITOR'S OWN CANVAS (§11.6). Not a schematic, not a sketch and not
                         a picture of a layout — LayoutCanvas itself, so the board pans and zooms by
                         exactly the gestures, the step sizes and the anchoring the layout editor uses.
                         Someone who has learned one of these windows has learned the other, and a
                         near-miss is worse than an absence because it is discovered by being wrong.

                         BRIEF 8 OWNS IT ENTIRELY. Brief 7 hosts it and wires the strip above; every
                         gesture and every pixel of overlay is brief 8's, through the CanvasOverlay
                         seam wBond already uses. -->
                    <Border Grid.Row="1" Name="BoardCard" Classes="card" Margin="0"
                            ClipToBounds="True" IsVisible="{Binding ShowBoard}">
                        <!-- THE LAYOUT EDITOR'S OWN RULERS, the same control (owner, 2026-09-19).
                             Every coordinate this window prints — a source, a load, a part's
                             placement, the readout under the cursor — is a place on this artwork, and
                             without a scale in the margin there was nothing on the picture to find it
                             against. LayoutRulerControl is driven entirely from code-behind and holds
                             no state of its own, so hosting it here is the same three calls the layout
                             editor makes and cannot drift from it.

                             Hidden with the canvas: the empty state's sentence is centred, and a
                             ruler frame around "no board yet" is chrome for a picture that is not
                             there. -->
                        <DockPanel>
                            <DockPanel Name="BoardRulerRow" DockPanel.Dock="Top" Height="22"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding HasBoard}">
                                <Border DockPanel.Dock="Left" Width="26"
                                        Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeMediumLowColor}"
                                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource SystemControlForegroundBaseLowBrush}"
                                        BorderThickness="0,0,1,1"/>
                                <ctl:LayoutRulerControl Name="BoardHRuler" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                                        ClipToBounds="True"/>
                            </DockPanel>
                            <ctl:LayoutRulerControl Name="BoardVRuler" DockPanel.Dock="Left" Width="26"
                                                    Orientation="Vertical" ClipToBounds="True"
                                                    IsVisible="{Binding HasBoard}"/>
                        <Panel>
                            <!-- HIDDEN UNTIL THERE IS A BOARD, because the map overlay draws a
                                 CENTRED note of its own ("No result yet. Run the rail.") and the
                                 placeholder below is centred too — so a window with nothing imported
                                 drew the two sentences on top of each other (owner, 2026-09-18). One
                                 empty state, and it is the one that says what to do next. -->
                            <!-- READ-ONLY. railRF SHOWS the board; the way to change the geometry is
                                 to open the `.clay` and edit it there, and watch this repaint
                                 (owner, 2026-09-18). Everything else about the control is unchanged —
                                 pan, zoom, F, Z, the zoom box and the overlay's own hit reading all
                                 work exactly as the layout editor's do, which is §11.6's whole rule. -->
                            <ctl:LayoutCanvas Name="BoardCanvas" ClipToBounds="True"
                                              ReadOnly="True"
                                              IsVisible="{Binding HasBoard}"
                                              ViewModel="{Binding BoardLayout}">
                                <!-- ONE ContextMenu instance, declared here and never constructed by
                                     the canvas (the L1-fix rule the layout editor's own view states:
                                     a right-click that built and opened its own menu stacked them).
                                     Its rows are rebuilt per opening, and railRF's Copy is one of
                                     them — R-rail9-6, through ILayoutCanvasOverlay's own seam. -->
                                <ctl:LayoutCanvas.ContextMenu>
                                    <ContextMenu Opening="OnBoardContextMenuOpening"/>
                                </ctl:LayoutCanvas.ContextMenu>
                            </ctl:LayoutCanvas>
                            <!-- §2.4: "a cursor reads out the absolute voltage anywhere on the net".
                                 Over the picture rather than beside it, because the number answers a
                                 question about the place the pointer is on. -->
                            <Border IsVisible="{Binding BoardReadout, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                                    Margin="6" Padding="6,2" CornerRadius="4"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1">
                                <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding BoardReadout}"/>
                            </Border>
                            <!-- WHERE THE POINTER IS, in the board's own display unit (owner,
                                 2026-09-19). The layout editor puts this in its status bar and this
                                 window had only the ruler ticks, so finding a place on the board by
                                 mouse meant counting them. Bottom RIGHT, opposite the value readout,
                                 because the two answer different questions about the same pointer
                                 and one box holding both would read as one sentence.

                                 The strings are LayoutEditorViewModel's own — the same property the
                                 layout editor binds — so the two windows cannot come to spell a
                                 coordinate differently. -->
                            <Border IsVisible="{Binding HasBoard}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                                    Margin="6" Padding="6,2" CornerRadius="4"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1">
                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="X:"/>
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding BoardLayout.CursorXText}"/>
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="Y:" Margin="6,0,0,0"/>
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding BoardLayout.CursorYText}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>
                            <TextBlock Classes="note" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding !HasBoard}"
                                       Text="No board yet. Import one — the artwork lands in the open workspace as an ordinary cell with a layout view."/>
                            <!-- ── §2.4's |Z| MAP, WITH ITS OWN CONTROLS (owner, 2026-09-19) ────
                                 The |Z| tab used to be a dead end: a sentence in railRF's own
                                 vocabulary ("it is the plane pair's own answer") followed by
                                 directions to a card on a DIFFERENT tab. Worse, a refused run
                                 leaves Plane null on purpose — the previous map is worth more
                                 than a cleared one — so the tab went on saying "no map yet" to
                                 somebody who had just pressed Find and been refused, with the
                                 refusal printed on the tab they had left.

                                 So the box, the button, the busy line and the refusal are here,
                                 on the tab that shows the picture. The Plane resonances card on
                                 the Frequency tab is unchanged and drives the same command — the
                                 MODE LIST is a frequency-tab answer and belongs beside the curve.

                                 The renderer's own centred note is suppressed while this shows
                                 (RailLayoutOverlay.EmptyNoteShownByHost); the sentence below is
                                 the same string, from RailMapScene.EmptyImpedanceNote, so the
                                 window and a headless render cannot come to spell it
                                 differently. -->
                            <Border IsVisible="{Binding ShowImpedanceFinder}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                    MaxWidth="420" Margin="16" Padding="14,12" CornerRadius="6"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="10">
                                    <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                         Classes.warn="{Binding HasPlaneRefusal}"
                                                         Text="{Binding ImpedanceFinderNote}"/>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="At"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding PlaneFrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The frequency the map is read at. It also sets the mesh, the copper's skin-effect resistance and the dielectric's loss, so the map and the modes are both of it. Opens on the top of this rail's band."/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="ImpedanceFindButton"
                                                Content="Find" FontSize="11" Padding="12,3"
                                                Command="{Binding PlaneResonancesCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Extract the plane pair at that frequency, solve its modes and map |Z| over it. Several seconds — it is never run automatically."/>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <!-- ── PROGRESS: STAGES AND AN INDETERMINATE BAR ─────────
                                         Never a percentage. The dominant cost is one dense LAPACK
                                         call — cubic in the cell count, no callbacks, nothing to
                                         subdivide — so a determinate bar would be animating a
                                         number nobody computed. The stage line carries what the
                                         run is doing AND how big the problem turned out to be,
                                         which is what actually explains the wait. -->
                                    <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding IsFindingModes}">
                                        <ProgressBar IsIndeterminate="True" Height="3"
                                                     Minimum="0" Maximum="1"/>
                                        <TextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                   Text="{Binding PlaneStage}"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── CHANGING THE FREQUENCY WITH A MAP UP (owner, 2026-09-19) ────
                                 The map is at ONE frequency, so the next thing anybody wants is
                                 another one. Hiding the controls the moment a map appeared left
                                 the Frequency tab's card as the only way back — the same "leave
                                 the picture to change the picture" the tab's own controls exist
                                 to end. A STRIP and not the centred panel: that one is an empty
                                 state and carries the sentence saying what the map is, which a
                                 reader looking at a map has already had answered. Top centre,
                                 because both bottom corners are readouts. -->
                            <Border IsVisible="{Binding ShowImpedanceRefind}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                                    Margin="6" Padding="8,3" CornerRadius="4"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="note" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                   Text="{Binding ImpedanceMapAt}"/>
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="re-run at"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="6,0,0,0"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Classes="val" Width="90"
                                                            Text="{Binding PlaneFrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="A new frequency for the map. The cavity is re-extracted at it, because the mesh, the copper's skin-effect resistance and the dielectric's loss are all of the frequency the map is read at."/>
                                        <Button Content="Find" FontSize="11" Padding="10,2"
                                                Command="{Binding PlaneResonancesCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Re-run the plane pair at that frequency."/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                    <StackPanel Spacing="3" IsVisible="{Binding IsFindingModes}">
                                        <ProgressBar IsIndeterminate="True" Height="3"
                                                     Minimum="0" Maximum="1"/>
                                        <TextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="360"
                                                   Text="{Binding PlaneStage}"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>
                        </Panel>
                        </DockPanel>
                    </Border>

                    <!-- ── The parts table (R-rail7-9) ─────────────────────────── -->
                    <Border Grid.Row="2" Name="PartsCard" Classes="card" Margin="0,5,0,0"
                            IsVisible="{Binding ShowParts}">
                        <Grid Name="PartsGrid" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" MaxHeight="170">
                            <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Parts"/>
                            <!-- Sortable column headers, LEFT-ALIGNED with the columns under them.
                                 This is R-rail7-2's one exception and it is an owner decision: any
                                 centring here is a misalignment the user can see. -->
                            <Grid Grid.Row="1" Name="PartsHeader"
                                  ColumnDefinitions="60,*,70,70,90,70,60,96">
                                <Button Classes="gridhdr" Content="refdes"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="gridhdr" Content="part number"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="2" Classes="gridhdr" Content="marked"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="3" Classes="gridhdr" Content="derated"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="4" Classes="gridhdr" Content="model source"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="5" Classes="gridhdr" Content="ESR"/>
                                <Button Grid.Column="6" Classes="gridhdr" Content="L mount"/>
                                <!-- WHERE IT IS, or that nothing says. Without this column the table
                                     was a list of parts with no visible relationship to the artwork
                                     beside it, and a reader looking for C1 on the board had nothing
                                     to tell them that railRF does not know where C1 is either
                                     (owner, 2026-09-19). -->
                                <Button Grid.Column="7" Classes="gridhdr" Content="where"/>
                            </Grid>
                            <!-- MaxHeight on the LIST, as every other card does it. The Grid's own
                                 MaxHeight constrains the MEASURE and does not clip, so a list taller
                                 than it simply draws past the card and over the status strip — which
                                 nobody saw while this pane was empty (R-rail18-5). -->
                            <!-- SELECTION IS THE LINK TO THE BOARD (owner, 2026-09-19). Picking a
                                 row outlines that part on the artwork beside it; Escape clears both.
                                 The row's own highlight is the ListBoxItem:selected style above. -->
                            <ListBox Grid.Row="2" Name="PartsList" Classes="rows" MaxHeight="128"
                                     ItemsSource="{Binding Parts}"
                                     SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedPart, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="rvm:RailPartRowViewModel">
                                        <!-- An unresolved row is rendered AS unresolved and populates
                                             no numeric column — a half-filled row is the shape a
                                             defaulted one takes, and the whole point of listing it is
                                             that it is not one. -->
                                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="60,*,70,70,90,70,60,96" Margin="0,1"
                                              Classes.unresolved="{Binding IsUnresolved}">
                                            <!-- WHERE THE ROW CAME FROM, on the row's own face
                                                 (R-rail18-5a) — typed on the rail, or pre-filled
                                                 from the bill of materials. A pre-filled number
                                                 nobody checked is the one that will be wrong. -->
                                            <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="{Binding Refdes}"
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                                       ToolTip.Tip="{Binding OriginText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding PartNumber}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding MarkedText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding DeratedText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding ModelSourceText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="5" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding EsrText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="6" Classes="detailLabel" 
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding MountingInductanceText}"/>
                                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="7" Classes="detailLabel"
                                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" Text="{Binding PositionText}"
                                                       ToolTip.Tip="{Binding PositionTooltip}"/>
                                        </Grid>
                                    </DataTemplate>
                                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                            </ListBox>
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>
                </Grid>
            </Border>

            <!-- ══ RESULTS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <Border Grid.Column="2" Name="ResultsPane" Classes="pane"
                    IsVisible="{Binding ShowResults}">
                <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto">
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                        <TextBlock Classes="panelhdr" Text="Results" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Name="ResultsTabStrip" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                    Spacing="2" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,0,0,6">
                            <!-- The same toolbar shape as the board panel's four, for the same
                                 reason (owner, 2026-09-19). SineWave is the LAYOUT EDITOR's own EM
                                 glyph — the one place in the application that already means "over
                                 frequency" — so it is spent here rather than on the |Z| MAP beside
                                 it, which is a picture of a place and wears the ohm sign. -->
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="DcTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="DC — the drop, the ranked breakdown and the via flags">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="CurrentDc" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="FrequencyTab"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Frequency — |Z| against the target, the anti-resonances and what the aggressors land on">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="SineWave" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                            <!-- ── THE READOUTS TOGGLE (owner, 2026-09-19) ─────────────────────
                                 The DC and frequency readouts under the plot can take a sizable
                                 share of this column, and a reader studying the curve wants that
                                 share back. Off, the plot's own ceiling is lifted and it takes the
                                 height they were using — RailRfWindow.CapResultsPlot owns that
                                 arithmetic, because a plot in an Auto row is offered an infinite
                                 height and the cap is the only thing holding it.

                                 SEPARATED BY A RULE from the two tabs beside it, because it is a
                                 different kind of control: those pick WHICH answer, this one
                                 changes how much of the panel the picture gets. Lit while the
                                 readouts are showing, on Button.ToolActive's accent — the one lamp
                                 this window uses everywhere. Not one of the four panel lamps: it
                                 divides this column rather than the window, so it has no last-one-
                                 showing rule and never dims. -->
                            <Border Width="1" VerticalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="3,2"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource SystemControlForegroundBaseLowBrush}"/>
                            <Button Classes="tabbtn" Name="ResultTextButton"
                                    Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ShowResultText}"
                                    Command="{Binding ToggleResultTextCommand}"
                                    ToolTip.Tip="Readouts — show or hide the text under the plot. Off, the plot takes the height they were using; every number is still there when they come back.">
                                <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="CardTextOutline" Width="14" Height="14"/>
                            </Button>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>

                    <!-- The plot is a PlotControl in RECTANGULAR mode, fed from a DataSet — never a
                         bespoke chart (§11.1). Brief 12 builds the DataSet and the traces; the host is
                         a DataDisplayViewModel with one container in it, which is what makes the
                         markers, the info boxes, the theme and the clipboard the Data Display's own
                         rather than four things this window would otherwise answer "nothing" to. -->
                    <!-- ── AND ITS HEIGHT IS CAPPED FROM THE CODE-BEHIND (owner, 2026-09-19)
                         An AspectRatioPanel in an Auto row is offered an INFINITE height, so its
                         height is whatever the width divides to — which was fine while this column
                         was always 340 px and became a bug the moment a panel toggle could hand it
                         the whole window: at 1,100 px wide the golden plot is 680 px tall, and it
                         took the drop, the breakdown and the via check off the bottom of the pane
                         with it, along with its own lower half. RailRfWindow.CapResultsPlot keeps
                         it under a share of the pane's own height; the panel then letterboxes,
                         which is what it does in any bounded row. -->
                    <ctl:AspectRatioPanel Grid.Row="1" Name="ImpedancePlotHost" Margin="0,2">
                        <!-- ── THE PLOT INSPECTOR OPENS HERE (owner, 2026-09-19) ──────────────
                             Double-click empty plot area and the Data Display's own inspector
                             flyout opens, which is where the Y unit lives: the curves are dBΩ and a
                             reader who wants ohms picks Mag on a trace card. The MASK and the
                             aggressor lines follow it without being cards of their own — they are
                             annotation, and RailRfViewModel.OnInspectorChangedThePlot is what
                             carries the choice to them.

                             CanDeletePlot stays False and Plot.IsFixedReadout hides the Add and
                             the per-card trash: every trace here is rebuilt from the sweep on each
                             re-solve, so a trace added would be gone by the next keystroke and one
                             removed would be back. What the panel CAN change — the unit, the
                             colour, the line — is carried across those rebuilds, markers with it. -->
                        <ddc:PlotControl Name="ImpedancePlotControl"
                                         Plot="{Binding ImpedancePlot}"
                                         Library="{Binding PlotHost.Library}"
                                         CanDeletePlot="False" CanEditPlotProperties="True"/>
                    </ctl:AspectRatioPanel>

                    <!-- A DOCKED LIST PANEL, not a modal sheet (§11.1) — so a user can click through
                         rows and watch the map and the curve change. Hidden by the Readouts toggle
                         above; hiding it collapses this star row to nothing, which is what lets the
                         plot's raised ceiling actually be used. -->
                    <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2" IsVisible="{Binding ShowResultText}"
                                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="0">
                            <!-- ── FIRST, and §9 is explicit about why ──────────────────────
                                 "The stackup is usually wrong. Designers copy a stackup from the
                                 last board. … railRF shows the extracted plane capacitance as a
                                 single number EARLY AND PROMINENTLY, because a designer recognises
                                 a wrong one instantly and would never notice it buried in a curve."
                                 One glance at it checks the permittivity, the overlap area and the
                                 dielectric thickness at once. It is also on the status strip. -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowStackupCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Stackup"/>
                                    <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                         Text="{Binding PlaneCapacitanceLine}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowDropCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Drop"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding PortLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowBreakdownCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Breakdown"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding BreakdownLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowViaCheckCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Via check"/>
                                    <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                         Text="{Binding ViaFlagSummary}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── The frequency answer (§2.4 "Over frequency") ──────────────
                                 THE COINCIDENCE LIST IS FIRST, and it is first on purpose: §2.4
                                 calls it "the sentence the tool exists to produce". A 9 dB peak
                                 nothing excites is not a problem and a 3 dB peak on the converter's
                                 fifth harmonic is, so the list that pairs the two outranks the table
                                 that merely locates the peaks. -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowCoincidencesCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="On an aggressor line"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding CoincidenceLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowMaskCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Against the target"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="120" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding MaskLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowAntiResonancesCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Anti-resonances"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding AntiResonanceLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowRemovalCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="If this part came off"/>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding RemovalLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- ── §2.4's plane resonances (P2b) ────────────────────────────
                                 "The cavity modes of your actual shape with a field map each, and
                                 |Z| across the whole plane at a chosen frequency. A MODE WHOSE
                                 MAXIMUM SITS ON THE LOAD PIN FIELD IS A PROBLEM; the same mode with
                                 its maximum in a corner is not, and only the map distinguishes
                                 them." So every row here names a frequency AND a place, and the map
                                 it goes with is the board panel's |Z| tab.

                                 ITS OWN BUTTON, for §2.9's own reason — the same reason Accuracy is
                                 one. The mode solve is dense and cubic in the cell count: tens of
                                 seconds on a real board, against milliseconds for the curve. The
                                 Fast edit loop re-solves on every committed row edit and this can
                                 never be in it. -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowPlaneResonancesCard}">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Plane resonances"/>
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel rowlbl" Text="At"/>
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            Text="{Binding PlaneFrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The frequency the cavity is read at. It sets the mesh (λ/20 there), the copper's skin-effect resistance and the dielectric's loss — so the modes and the |Z| map are both of it. Opens on the top of this rail's band."/>
                                        <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="PlaneResonancesButton"
                                                Content="Find" FontSize="11" Padding="10,2"
                                                Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                                Command="{Binding PlaneResonancesCommand}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Extract the plane pair at that frequency and solve its modes. Tens of seconds on a real board — it is never run automatically."/>
                                    </Grid>
                                    <StackPanel Spacing="3" IsVisible="{Binding IsFindingModes}">
                                        <ProgressBar IsIndeterminate="True" Height="3"
                                                     Minimum="0" Maximum="1"/>
                                        <TextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                   Text="{Binding PlaneStage}"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                    <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" IsVisible="{Binding HasModes}"
                                                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                                        <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                             Text="{Binding ModeLines, Converter={x:Static conv:RailLinesToTextConverter.Instance}}"/>
                                    </ScrollViewer>
                                    <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                         Text="{Binding PlaneMessage}"
                                                         IsVisible="{Binding HasPlaneMessage}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- What the frequency answer has to say that is not a curve — a refusal,
                                 or the notes and warnings that travel with the numbers, the
                                 INDICATIVE marking of §9 among them. -->
                            <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding ShowImpedanceMessageCard}">
                                <SelectableTextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding ImpedanceMessage}"
                                                     TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                            </Border>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </ScrollViewer>
                </Grid>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ── The status strip (§11.3's second point) ────────────────────
             ALWAYS ON SCREEN, so nobody reads a fast answer as an accurate one, and the elapsed time
             makes the cost of Accuracy obvious BEFORE it is pressed. A refusal appears here, WITH ITS
             NUMBERS IN IT, and turns the control that answers it red — the two are one mechanism, not
             a sentence plus a separate hunt. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="pane" Margin="4,0,4,0" Padding="10,4">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                <SelectableTextBlock Name="StatusStrip" Classes="note" Text="{Binding StatusLine}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding !HasRefusal}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                <SelectableTextBlock Name="RefusalStrip" Classes="warn" Text="{Binding Refusal.Sentence}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding HasRefusal}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="note" Text="both models in hand"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasBothModels}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           ToolTip.Tip="The fast curve stays on the plot beside the accurate one, so the error is measured on this design rather than promised in a document."/>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ── The bottom bar (§11.3's third point) ───────────────────────
             ACCURACY IS A BUTTON HERE, NEXT TO RUN — the one control that changes what the numbers
             mean, in the place the user looks when the design is settled. Never entered
             automatically, and never left silently. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="3" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="10,6">
            <!-- THE REASON RUN IS REFUSED IS NOT REPEATED HERE. RefreshRunGate turns every gate
                 reason into a Refusal, so the strip above already carries that exact sentence and
                 turns the control that answers it red — two rows of one sentence, one of them in the
                 warning colour, read as two different problems (owner, 2026-09-18). It is still on
                 the Run button's own tooltip, where it answers "why is this disabled". -->
            <!-- WHAT IT IS DOING, WHERE THE BUTTON IS. The status strip's "solving…" was the only
                 sign a run was in flight and it is at the far end of a wide window, so a press of
                 Accuracy — which takes roughly eight times the fast reading and moves the numbers by
                 under a millivolt on a good board — read as a dead button (owner, 2026-09-19). Both
                 buttons also disable while a solve is in flight, so the press is visibly taken. -->
            <TextBlock Classes="note" Text="{Binding BusyText}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsVisible="{Binding IsSolving}" Margin="0,0,10,0"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Right" Grid.Column="0"/>

            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                <!-- ACCURACY IS A TOOLBAR BUTTON AND IT IS LIT WHILE ITS READING IS ON SCREEN
                     (owner, 2026-09-19: "how do I know Accuracy is turned on? Shouldn't it be a
                     button that highlights when activated, kind of like toolbar glyphs?").

                     Square, icon-only and on Button.ToolActive's accent, which is the same lamp
                     every tool button in the layout editor wears. A lit BUTTON rather than a
                     ToggleButton because there is nothing to toggle off: Accuracy is a reading,
                     not a mode, and the lamp goes out by itself the moment an edit puts the Fast
                     answer back — which is §2.9's "never left silently" made visible.

                     The bullseye is the glyph because the button is named for what its answer IS,
                     not for the mesh that produces it; the mesh is in the tooltip, with its cost. -->
                <!-- ── ToolTip.Placement AND ToolTip.VerticalOffset, on every button of this row
                     THE OFFSET IS THE OTHER HALF, AND WITHOUT IT THE PLACEMENT DOES NOTHING. The
                     flash was reported again after Placement="Top" alone (owner, 2026-09-19), and
                     this time it was read out of the shipping Avalonia.Controls.dll rather than
                     argued from the symptom:

                       ToolTip.VerticalOffsetProperty is registered with a DEFAULT OF 20.0

                     — a sensible default for PlacementMode.Pointer, where it drops the popup clear
                     of the cursor. ManagedPopupPositioner adds that offset to the gravitated anchor
                     point unconditionally (`Gravitate(GetAnchorPoint(...), size, g) + offset`), so
                     under Placement="Top" it pushes the popup 20 px back DOWN, over the top 20 px of
                     a button that is about 32 px tall. The popup therefore still lands under the
                     pointer, the button still loses :pointerover to a different top-level,
                     ToolTipService still closes the tip, and BetweenShowDelay's 100 ms still makes
                     the re-open immediate. That is the loop, and it is worst on the tallest tooltip
                     in the row, which is this one.

                     -4 puts the popup fully above the button with a small gap. Placement alone is
                     not enough and the offset alone would be worse — together they take the pointer
                     out of the geometry entirely, wherever the window sits on screen. -->
                <Button Name="AccuracyButton" Padding="6,3"
                        Classes.ToolActive="{Binding IsShowingAccuracy}"
                        Classes.flagged="{Binding IsModelKindFlagged}"
                        Command="{Binding AccuracyCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Placement="Top" ToolTip.VerticalOffset="-4"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Accuracy — run the mesh solve. It lights while the accurate reading is on screen; press it again to go back to the fast one, and an edit does the same on its own. Never entered automatically and never left silently; both readings stay, so the two are compared on this board.">
                    <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Bullseye" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                </Button>
                <Button Name="CompareButton" Content="Compare…" FontSize="11" Padding="10,3"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanExport}"
                        ToolTip.Placement="Top" ToolTip.VerticalOffset="-4"
                        ToolTip.Tip="This design against another .crail — the one that is known to work. Both sides are solved at the model kind on screen, the same rail is matched by name, and the ports and parts pair by refdes and pin."/>
                <Button Name="ExportButton" Content="Export" FontSize="11" Padding="10,3"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanExport}"
                        ToolTip.Placement="Top" ToolTip.VerticalOffset="-4"
                        ToolTip.Tip="CSV, .npy, .mat, .txt, or the report page as SVG or PDF — the extension picks the format, exactly as circuitrf rail -o does, and both write the same bytes."/>
                <Button Name="RunButton" Content="Run" FontSize="11" Padding="14,3"
                        IsDefault="True"
                        Command="{Binding RunCommand}"
                        ToolTip.Placement="Top" ToolTip.VerticalOffset="-4"
                        ToolTip.Tip="{Binding RunBlockedReason}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ── The marker info-box overlay (owner, 2026-09-19) ────────────────────────────────
             A MARKER'S BOX HAD NOWHERE TO BE DRAWN. The last round gave this window's PlotControl
             every provider it asks its host for, so a double-click DOES add a marker and
             DataDisplayViewModel DOES build a MarkerInfoBoxViewModel for it — and nothing in the
             window rendered that collection, so the box existed and was invisible. This is the
             Match Designer's own overlay, item for item, and it is the other half of that wiring.

             IT SPANS THE WHOLE WINDOW rather than the results pane. A box is DRAGGABLE and belongs
             to the display and not to the plot it was made from; the results column is 340 px wide
             and a box pinned inside it could not be moved off its own curve. Placed last so it
             renders above everything, and ROWSPAN across all four rows of the root grid.

             Background="{x:Null}" on both the ItemsControl and its Canvas: a null background is not
             hit-testable, so every click falls through to whatever is underneath, while each
             MarkerInfoBoxView sets Background="Transparent" and is hit-testable itself.

             RailRfWindow.SyncPlotContainers keeps ImpedanceContainer's Left/Top/Width/Height equal
             to the PlotControl's rectangle IN THIS PANEL'S COORDINATES, which is the space
             ViewLeft/ViewTop are in. -->
        <ItemsControl Name="MarkerInfoBoxLayer" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="4"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding PlotHost.MarkerInfoBoxes}"
                      Background="{x:Null}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <Canvas Background="{x:Null}"/>
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsControl.ItemContainerTheme>
                <ControlTheme TargetType="ContentPresenter">
                    <Setter Property="Canvas.Left" Value="{ReflectionBinding ViewLeft}"/>
                    <Setter Property="Canvas.Top"  Value="{ReflectionBinding ViewTop}"/>
                </ControlTheme>
            </ItemsControl.ItemContainerTheme>
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="ddv:MarkerInfoBoxViewModel">
                    <ddvw:MarkerInfoBoxView/>
                </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
    </Grid>
</Window>
